WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.448
TypeScript improves clarity and maintainability, but security still depends on runtime controls and disciplined engineering.

2
00:00:08.448 --> 00:00:13.132
Treat external data as untrusted until it has been parsed and validated.

3
00:00:13.132 --> 00:00:21.350
Use unknown, schema validation, parsing functions, and narrow application types at boundaries where data enters the system.

4
00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:25.651
Avoid unsafe type escapes unless they are justified and reviewed.

5
00:00:25.651 --> 00:00:30.566
any, unchecked assertions, and non-null assertions can hide uncertainty.

6
00:00:30.566 --> 00:00:43.698
Model data carefully, validate shape and meaning, normalize values before sensitive use, and remember that a value with the right type may still be unauthorized or inappropriate for the current action.

7
00:00:43.698 --> 00:00:48.383
Keep secrets and authorization decisions in trusted server-side code.

8
00:00:48.383 --> 00:00:53.912
Client-side checks can improve usability, but the server must enforce permissions.

9
00:00:53.912 --> 00:01:02.360
API handlers should validate requests, preserve invariants on failure, return safe errors, and avoid logging sensitive values.

10
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:06.661
Dependencies and build settings should be managed deliberately.

11
00:01:06.661 --> 00:01:13.649
Finally, reinforce good habits with tests, review, linting, CI gates, release notes, and patch processes.

12
00:01:13.649 --> 00:01:17.259
A secure TypeScript application is not just well typed.

13
00:01:17.259 --> 00:01:24.862
It is designed so trust boundaries are explicit, risky assumptions are visible, and safe behavior is the normal path.

14
00:01:24.862 --> 00:01:31.927
Type clarity is a strong starting point, but runtime trust has to be earned every time data crosses a boundary.
